</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Parameter-efficient fine-tuning with PIZA</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To perform fine-tuning with the PIZA module, we incorporate the embeddings <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}</annotation></semantics></math> into parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods.
Here, we propose prompt-based, LoRA-based and adapter-based fine-tuning with PIZA. Their architectures are shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Progressive-integrative zooming adapter ‣ 4 Method ‣ Referring Expression Comprehension for Small Objects" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PIZA-CoOp.</span>
CoOp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite> is a prompt-based fine-tuning method, which prepends learnable embeddings to the input text prompts as <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\bm{x},\bm{t})=F(\bm{x},[\bm{e},\bm{t}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒕</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\bm{x},\bm{t})=F(\bm{x},[\bm{e},\bm{t}])</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{e}=(\bm{e}_{0},\bm{e}_{1},\cdots,\bm{e}_{L})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒆</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{e}=(\bm{e}_{0},\bm{e}_{1},\cdots,\bm{e}_{L})</annotation></semantics></math> is a sequence of learnable embeddings. PIZA-CoOp inserts <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\hskip-1.0pt\text{\scalebox{0.56}{\raisebox{1.32611pt}{$\bigcirc$}}}\hskip-4.3pt{*}}(\bm{x},\bm{t})=F(\bm{x},[\bm{e},H(\bm{h}),\bm{t}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mpadded style="width:4.5pt;height:3.5pt;vertical-align:-0.8pt;" depth="0.8pt" height="2.7pt" voffset="1.3pt" width="4.5pt"><mo>○</mo></mpadded><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝒆</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒉</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒕</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\hskip-1.0pt\text{\scalebox{0.56}{\raisebox{1.32611pt}{$\bigcirc$}}}\hskip-4.3pt{*}}(\bm{x},\bm{t})=F(\bm{x},[\bm{e},H(\bm{h}),\bm{t}])</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable linear layer as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Progressive-integrative zooming adapter ‣ 4 Method ‣ Referring Expression Comprehension for Small Objects" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></span> (a)</a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PIZA-LoRA.</span>
LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> is a low-rank adaptation method that injects trainable low-rank matrices into linear projections as <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\bm{x}+BA\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\bm{x}+BA\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is an input, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is a frozen weight matrix and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable low-rank matrices.
PIZA-LoRA integrates the embeddings <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}</annotation></semantics></math> into the bottleneck of LoRA as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Progressive-integrative zooming adapter ‣ 4 Method ‣ Referring Expression Comprehension for Small Objects" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></span> (b)</a>, resulting in <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\bm{x}+BA\bm{x}+BC\bm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒙</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒉</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\bm{x}+BA\bm{x}+BC\bm{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a newly added learnable matrix. We set the rank to <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PIZA-Adapter+.</span>
Adapter+ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> utilizes the post-adapter architecture, channel-wise scaling and Houlsby initialization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. PIZA-Adapter+ adds the embeddings <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{h}</annotation></semantics></math> to the output of the channel-wise scaling layer as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2 Progressive-integrative zooming adapter ‣ 4 Method ‣ Referring Expression Comprehension for Small Objects" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></span> (c)</a>. We set the bottleneck dimension to 256.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Training</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a training dataset <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of images, expressions and ground truth bounding boxes, we construct an extended dataset <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> that involves ground truth search processes for training with PIZA.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview.</span>
Each ground truth search process <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> represents zooming steps to localize the target object.
Specifically, it is given by <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{*}=(\bm{b}_{0}^{*},\bm{b}_{1}^{*},\cdots,\bm{b}_{T^{*}}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{*}=(\bm{b}_{0}^{*},\bm{b}_{1}^{*},\cdots,\bm{b}_{T^{*}}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{0}^{*}=(0,0,W,H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{0}^{*}=(0,0,W,H)</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the entire image and <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{T^{*}}^{*}=\bm{b}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{T^{*}}^{*}=\bm{b}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ground truth bounding box.
To facilitate efficient fine-tuning,
we generate <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> so that the distribution of inverse zoom factors <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z^{*}_{j})^{-1}=|\bm{b}^{*}_{j}|/|\bm{b}^{*}_{j-1}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z^{*}_{j})^{-1}=|\bm{b}^{*}_{j}|/|\bm{b}^{*}_{j-1}|</annotation></semantics></math> match to the distribution of bounding box area ratios <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(r)</annotation></semantics></math> in pre-training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distribution <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p(r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p(r)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
Along with a pre-trained model, the distribution <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(r)</annotation></semantics></math> is pre-estimated by applying kernel density estimation to the pre-training dataset (we use the union of O365 and GoldG in our experiments).
We randomly sample 100,000 bounding boxes to compute area ratios <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=|\bm{b}^{*}|/(WH)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=|\bm{b}^{*}|/(WH)</annotation></semantics></math> and apply a Gaussian kernel.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Width and height for <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b_{j}^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b_{j}^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> .</span>
The width and height for each bounding box in <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are randomly sampled in three steps.
First, ratios <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are randomly sampled from <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(r)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,2,\cdots,T_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,2,\cdots,T_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sufficiently large constant.
Second, the number of zooming steps <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined such that the cumulative product of <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is closest the area ratio <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of the ground truth bounding box:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T^{*}=\mathop{\rm argmin}\limits_{T}\left(\frac{1}{r^{*}}\prod_{k=1}^{T}r_{k}-1\right),\ r^{*}=\frac{|\bm{b}^{*}|}{WH}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">argmin</mo><mi>T</mi></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T^{*}=\mathop{\rm argmin}\limits_{T}\left(\frac{1}{r^{*}}\prod_{k=1}^{T}r_{k}-1\right),\ r^{*}=\frac{|\bm{b}^{*}|}{WH},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bounding box sampled from <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W,H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W,H</annotation></semantics></math> are the width and height of the corresponding image.
Then, the zoom factors <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and the size of bounding boxes <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are determined as follow:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z_{j}^{*}\hskip-3.0pt=\hskip-3.0pt\left(\frac{1}{r^{*}}\prod_{k=1}^{T^{*}}r_{k}^{\omega_{k}^{-1}}\right)^{\hskip-3.0pt\frac{1}{T^{*}}}\hskip-9.0ptr_{j}^{-1},\,S_{j}^{*}\hskip-3.0pt=\hskip-3.0pt\left(\prod_{k=1}^{T^{*}-j}z_{T^{*}-k}\right)|\bm{b}^{*}|," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z_{j}^{*}\hskip-3.0pt=\hskip-3.0pt\left(\frac{1}{r^{*}}\prod_{k=1}^{T^{*}}r_{k}^{\omega_{k}^{-1}}\right)^{\hskip-3.0pt\frac{1}{T^{*}}}\hskip-9.0ptr_{j}^{-1},\,S_{j}^{*}\hskip-3.0pt=\hskip-3.0pt\left(\prod_{k=1}^{T^{*}-j}z_{T^{*}-k}\right)|\bm{b}^{*}|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}=\lambda_{1}e^{-\lambda_{2}{k}}/\sum_{k^{\prime}=1}^{T^{*}}\lambda_{1}e^{-\lambda_{2}{k^{\prime}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}=\lambda_{1}e^{-\lambda_{2}{k}}/\sum_{k^{\prime}=1}^{T^{*}}\lambda_{1}e^{-\lambda_{2}{k^{\prime}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are weights derived from an exponential distribution.
This weighting ensures that <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}^{*}\simeq r^{-1}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}^{*}\simeq r^{-1}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with smaller <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, encouraging more precise bounding box predictions at the initial zooming step.
Finally, the width and height of <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{j}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{j}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are determined by
<math id="S4.SS4.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{*}_{j}=a_{j}\sqrt{S^{*}_{j}},\quad h^{*}_{j}=a_{j}^{-1}\sqrt{S^{*}_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{*}_{j}=a_{j}\sqrt{S^{*}_{j}},\quad h^{*}_{j}=a_{j}^{-1}\sqrt{S^{*}_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is an interpreted aspect ratio</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a_{j}=\frac{W}{H}-\left(\frac{W}{H}-1\right)\frac{WH-S^{*}_{j}}{WH-S^{*}_{T^{*}-1}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>W</mi><mi>H</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>W</mi><mi>H</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a_{j}=\frac{W}{H}-\left(\frac{W}{H}-1\right)\frac{WH-S^{*}_{j}}{WH-S^{*}_{T^{*}-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Centers.</span>
The center of each bounding box is aligned with the center coordinates of the target object. If the region extends beyond the image boundaries, the bounding box is minimally shifted to remain entirely within the image.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Labels.</span>
Finally, for the generated search process <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the sequence of binary labels <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{y}^{*}=(y^{*}_{0},y^{*}_{1},\cdots,y^{*}_{T^{*}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{y}^{*}=(y^{*}_{0},y^{*}_{1},\cdots,y^{*}_{T^{*}})</annotation></semantics></math> and zooming step labels <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z=(z^{*}_{0},z^{*}_{1},\cdots,z^{*}_{T^{*}},)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>z</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(z^{*}_{0},z^{*}_{1},\cdots,z^{*}_{T^{*}},)</annotation></semantics></math> are attached. Each label is given by</p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y^{*}_{j}=\begin{cases}\texttt{[CONT]}&amp;(0\leq j&lt;T^{*})\\
\texttt{[EOS]}&amp;(j=T^{*})\\
\end{cases},\quad z_{j}^{*}=\frac{j}{T^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[CONT]</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[EOS]</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>j</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y^{*}_{j}=\begin{cases}\texttt{[CONT]}&amp;(0\leq j&lt;T^{*})\\
\texttt{[EOS]}&amp;(j=T^{*})\\
\end{cases},\quad z_{j}^{*}=\frac{j}{T^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loss function.</span>
The loss for fine-tuning is computed in three steps.
First, a mini-batch of quadruplets <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{x},\bm{t},P^{*},\bm{y}^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{x},\bm{t},P^{*},\bm{y}^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Second, for each quadruplet, index <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2,\cdots,T^{*}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2,\cdots,T^{*}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is randomly drawn to compute the forward process <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\bm{b}}_{i+1}=F_{\hskip-1.0pt\text{\scalebox{0.56}{\raisebox{1.32611pt}{$\bigcirc$}}}\hskip-4.3pt{*}}(\bm{x},\bm{t},\bm{b}_{0:i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mpadded style="width:4.5pt;height:3.5pt;vertical-align:-0.8pt;" depth="0.8pt" height="2.7pt" voffset="1.3pt" width="4.5pt"><mo>○</mo></mpadded><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{b}}_{i+1}=F_{\hskip-1.0pt\text{\scalebox{0.56}{\raisebox{1.32611pt}{$\bigcirc$}}}\hskip-4.3pt{*}}(\bm{x},\bm{t},\bm{b}_{0:i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, the loss depending on the pre-trained model is applied. In the experiments, we implement PIZA over the GroundingDINO model; thus the loss consists of the contrastive loss and the localization loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
